{
  "id": "proj-hand-forged-wall-hooks",
  "slug": "hand-forged-wall-hooks",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Hand-Forged Wall Hooks",
  "summary": "A set of scrolled coat hooks. Teaches tapering, scrolling and punching holes hot.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 3,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Set the forge up outdoors or in a well-ventilated space, light it, and cut the 1/4 in square bar into four 10 in lengths with the hacksaw.",
    "Heat 2 in of one end to bright orange and draw a 2 in taper on the anvil face, turning the bar 90 degrees between blows to keep it square. Stop hammering when the colour drops to dull red and reheat.",
    "Scroll the tapered tip into a small curl by bending it back over the edge of the anvil face, then closing it on the face. This curl is the tip of the hook.",
    "Flatten the opposite 3 in into a 3/4 in wide mounting plate. Hot-punch two screw holes over the anvil's pritchel hole: punch most of the way through, flip the bar, and drive the slug out from the back.",
    "Heat the middle of the bar and bend it over the anvil horn so the curl stands about 2 in out from the plate. Check that all four hooks match before they cool.",
    "Wire-brush off the scale while the steel is still hot, let it cool until wax melts on it without smoking, and rub on paste wax for a black, rust-resistant finish. Mount with black pan-head screws into studs."
  ],
